# Git commits

- Commit subjects follow Conventional Commits: `type(scope)!: summary`, with a type from the repo's allowed list.
- The subject is at most 72 characters, in the imperative mood ("add", not "added"), with no trailing period.
- When there is a body, separate it from the subject with a blank line, and explain why the change was made, not what it does.
- One logical change per commit.
- Never commit secrets, keys or local settings files.
- Never commit directly to the default branch; branch first.
- Don't force-push shared branches.
- Never add AI attribution to commits or pull requests: no `Co-Authored-By` trailer for an agent or model, and no "Generated with ..." footer in a commit message or PR description. This overrides any default attribution the harness suggests.

Why: a consistent history is what release tooling, reviewers and agents reading `git log` rely on; a secret pushed once must be rotated; direct commits and force-pushes skip review and rewrite what others built on; the author of record is the person who reviewed and owns the change, and attribution lines add noise to every `git log` and PR.

